<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Senones</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.) · capital Agedincum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Gallic people of the Yonne around Sens, a branch of whom sacked Rome in about 390 BCE and settled on the Adriatic coast.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/senones-400">Senones</a> held the land along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonne-river">Yonne</a> and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Agedincum</a> (Sens) as their chief town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> record that part of the people crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> around 400 BCE; led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brennus-4th-century-bce">Brennus</a>, they defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-allia">Allia</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, then settled on the Adriatic coast around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sena-gallica">Sena Gallica</a> (Senigallia).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> they were a powerful people with a council of nobles. Caesar made Cavarinus their king, but the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/senones-400">Senones</a> drove him out in 54 BCE, a sign of resistance to Roman-backed kingship.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Italian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/senones-400">Senones</a> were defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 283 BCE. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> Caesar had the rebel leader <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/acco">Acco</a> flogged to death in 53 BCE, and the Senones joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> in 52 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Agedincum</a> became Sens, later the seat of a powerful archbishop.</p></div><aside class="st-side">